<div class="mx-auto flex max-w-360 gap-10 px-5 sm:px-8 lg:px-10">
  <%= render "showcase/sidebar" %>
  <main id="main" class="min-w-0 max-w-4xl flex-1 py-5 lg:py-10">
    <%= render "showcase/mobile_navigation" %>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-12 pt-6 lg:pt-0">
      <header class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h1 class="text-balance text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Installation</h1>
        <p class="text-pretty text-ink-2">Set up the gem once. Then call any component directly from ERB.</p>
      </header>
      <section id="requirements" class="flex scroll-mt-28 flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Before you start</h2>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">Use Rails 8 or later with Propshaft, importmap, Stimulus and <code>tailwindcss-rails</code> 4.4 or later. Tailwind CSS v4 is required: it compiles the component styles and the utilities in your views. A Rails app created with Tailwind includes this setup.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="gem" class="flex scroll-mt-28 flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">1. Add the gem</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-ink-2">Add this to your <code>Gemfile</code>, then run <code>bundle install</code>.</p>
        <%= render "showcase/source_code", source: 'gem "lostcoords-ui", github: "lostcoords/ui"', language: :ruby, id: "install-gem", label: "Gemfile entry" %>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">The engine makes all <code>ui_*</code> helpers available to views. There are no per-component Ruby or JavaScript imports. Commit <code>Gemfile.lock</code> to pin the revision your app uses.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="styles" class="flex scroll-mt-28 flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">2. Add the stylesheet</h2>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">For a new app, save the <a href="/starter.css" download="application.css" class="underline underline-offset-2">starter stylesheet</a> as <code>app/assets/tailwind/application.css</code>. The engine import includes the default light and dark theme.</p>
        <%= render "showcase/source_code", source: DocsInstallation.stylesheet, language: :css, id: "install-starter", label: "Starter stylesheet" %>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">For an existing app, add the starter's layer order and typography plugin if needed. Keep your Tailwind imports and add the engine import:</p>
        <%= render "showcase/source_code", source: '@import "../builds/tailwind/lostcoords_ui.css";', language: :css, id: "install-styles", label: "Engine stylesheet import" %>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">Run <code>bin/rails tailwindcss:build</code>. The build generates that import path from the installed gem. Components work with the defaults immediately. To customize colors or fonts, add overrides after the import using the <a href="/theming.html" class="underline underline-offset-2">theming guide</a>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="javascript" class="flex scroll-mt-28 flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">3. Load the assets</h2>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">Your layout needs the compiled Tailwind stylesheet and importmap tags. Keep your existing tags if they already load these assets.</p>
        <%= render "showcase/source_code", source: DocsInstallation::LAYOUT, id: "install-layout", label: "Layout asset tags" %>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">The engine adds its Stimulus pins to the importmap. Rails’ standard <code>eagerLoadControllersFrom("controllers", application)</code> registration loads them with your app’s controllers.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="first-component" class="flex scroll-mt-28 flex-col gap-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">4. Use a component</h2>
        <%= render "showcase/source_code", source: DocsInstallation::FIRST_COMPONENT, id: "install-first-component", label: "First component" %>
        <p class="text-pretty text-sm text-ink-2">Helpers render native HTML. Use <code>class:</code> for additional utilities, <code>data:</code> and <code>aria:</code> for attributes, and blocks for custom content. Browse the <a href="/components/button.html" class="underline underline-offset-2">button examples</a> to see variants, sizes, links and Rails forms.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</div>
